      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190413/assets/f43c53ce3804de0ec59ca135a97f11e50f1022c129d87f4ea0df7bce4665d130.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2+1)-g(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(3)-g(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{1} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
